Dr. Tom Blinn
Phil Farrell
Steve VanDevender
Oisin McGuinness
Yaacov Fenster
Sean McInerney
Donald Rye
John Becker
Pam Woods

There are three answers:

1) The /proc directory and its contents do not use any disk space since it
pertains to memory.

2) Even if the /proc files used any disk space, they would overload the
/proc filesystems rather than the root filesystem since /proc is it's own
mount point.  That is obvious and I was in a hurry for a solution so I
overlooked that.

3) The space hogging culprit is somewhere in the root file system (I knew
that, just assumed /proc was part of the root file system which it's not
since it has it's own mount point).  The main suggestions were to look in
the /tmp and /dev subdirectories.

When I looked in my /tmp directory, I found huge files that were indeed the
cause of the / filesystem overload so I deleted them and all is well.

-----------------------------------
I run DEC unix V4.0B on a DEC alpha server 1000.  As indicated below, my
root file system is overloaded:

