X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96078654410623


First a brief history:
---------------------

The system is an AlphaServer 2100 4/200 with 192 Mb Ram, which was
upgraded from Digital Unix 2.0 to 4.0b in March 1997. 

The machine's primary purpose in life is to be a news server, using Inn.
Around the same time as the OS upgrade, NNTP feeder software was 
upgraded from nntplink to innfeed.

The news spools are on UFS file systems, split (fairly evenly) between
two SCSI controllers. Most everything else, (except / ) is on AdvFS.

The problem:
-----------

The system is not performing up to our expectations. Since the OS
upgrade in March, the cpu spends most of its time in system time. Idle
time is consistently near 0%. User time has increased, but not
dramatically. Essentially, we lost the cpu's idle time to system time
when the OS was upgraded.

Our attempts to track this down:
-------------------------------

We added an 8 Mb Prestoserve board, hoping to increase the performance
of the disk subsystem. Note that we only cache the news spools on the
Prestoserve board.

We switched the history database from AdvFS to UFS.

We altered our expiry runs from three times daily to twice daily during
